.TH CAMSRV 4
.SH NAME
camsrv \- a camera as a live video feed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount {camsrv} /mnt/video
.br
.B mount {camsrv -t} /mnt/video
.br
.B mount {camsrv -l
.I file
.B } /mnt/video
.br
.B mount {camsrv -r
.I degrees
.B } /mnt/video
.br
.B vm 1 camsrv -s
.I spec
.br
.B mount -A '#s\fIspec\fP/fs' /mnt/video
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Camsrv
serves a Raspberry Pi Camera Module 3 in the schema of
.IR vid9p ,
so what shows a video feed shows a camera, here or across a mount:
.BR wm/camera ,
.BR vidplay9p ,
and Matrix's
.BR video-pane .
With
.B -t
it serves a simulated sensor, on any machine: colour bars and a moving
band, as raw samples, through the same code.
.PP
With
.B -s
it serves no standard input: it posts itself in
.BI #s spec /fs
.RI ( post9p (2))
and serves each that opens that file, as a connection of its own, so the
camera can run in one Dis VM
.RI ( vm (1))
and be mounted from any other, on another core.
Every connection sees the one camera: frame
.I N
is the same frame on all of them, and the sensor runs while any of them
reads it.
.PP
With
.B -l
it appends its
.B status
line to
.IR file ,
seconds since boot first, once a minute, which is how a soak reads it
from outside the namespace the feed is mounted in.
.PP
With
.BR -r ,
the picture is turned clockwise by
.IR degrees ,
0, 90, 180 or 270, for a camera mounted on its side or upside down;
the same as
.B rotate
below, from the start. How the camera is mounted is a fact about the
installation, so it is whoever starts
.I camsrv
that says so, not the readers: each of them gets the picture the right
way up, sized as turned. On a bare-metal Pi 4 the card's
.B camera
file is where that is said: the system starts
.I camsrv
on
.B /mnt/video
at boot and writes the file's lines to
.BR ctl .
.PP
.I Camsrv
is the only thing that knows the sensor. It drives it over
.IR i2c (3),
takes raw frames from
.IR camera (3),
and turns them into pictures with
.BR $Bayer .
The sensor is on only while something has
.B frame
or
.B raw
open, and the last close powers it down.
.TP
.B 0/fmt
.RI `` w
.I h
.B i420
.IR fps ''.
.TP
.B 0/frame
I420, full range, numbered as
.IR vid9p 's
frames are: frame
.I N
is the frame size's bytes at offset
.IR N ×framesize,
the same for every reader, so a record keyed
.BI frame= N
.RI ( layers (2))
means the same frame to all of them.
The last eight frames are kept; a read below them is refused with
.LR "frame expired" ,
and a read past the newest waits for that frame.
A live reader starts at the newest
.RB ( status 's
.B frames=
less one, if that is not below
.BR first= )
and goes on from the newest again when it falls behind.
Numbers run on across the sensor going off and on.
.TP
.B 0/raw
A frame as the sensor gave it, 16 bits a sample, little-endian, at the
size and mosaic in
.BR status .
.TP
.B 0/status
One line of
.IB key = value\f1:
.B frames=
(one past the newest frame) and
.B first=
(the oldest kept), and no
.BR pos= :
this is what marks a feed live to its readers.
.BR autofocus=
says how the last run went.
.TP
.B 0/layer/
Empty as served: where what is drawn over the frames is bound, a file a
layer, stacked in the union's order
.RI ( layers (2)).
.TP
.B 0/ctl
Writes, one at a time; reading gives the settings.
.RS
.PD 0
.TP
.BI mode " w h
Picture size: 576 324 or 1152 648, or 324 576 or 648 1152 turned a
quarter. Not while streaming.
.TP
.BI fps " n
1 to 56. Not while streaming.
.TP
.BI exposure " lines
0, the default, is automatic.
.TP
.BI gain " code
Analogue gain, 0 to 960, of 1024\-1024/\fIgain\fR.
.TP
.BI focus " n
The lens, 0 to 1023. Taken at any time, and applied when the sensor
starts.
.TP
.B focus auto
Contrast autofocus, once: a few seconds, needing a still picture with
detail in it. A picture that moves, or has nothing to focus on, leaves
the lens where it was, and
.B status
says which.
.TP
.BI flip " n
1 mirrors, 2 turns upside down. 3, the default, is right way up as the
module usually sits. This is the sensor's own register: it changes the
raw frames too. Not while streaming.
.TP
.BI rotate " degrees
Turn the picture clockwise: 0, 90, 180 or 270. A quarter turn swaps
.B fmt 's
width and height.
.B raw
is not turned. Not while streaming.
.PD
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
A video with known answers to point the camera at during a soak:
exposure steps, white balance, a focus chart, motion:
.IP
.B tools/camera-test-pattern.py loop.mp4
.PP
.I Camsrv
runs in a process group of its own. To stop it, unmount it; to kill
it, write
.B killgrp
to the
.B ctl
file of its process: killing one of its processes alone leaves the
mount without a server and its readers waiting.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/camsrv.b
.br
.B /libinterp/bayer.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR camera (3),
.IR i2c (3),
.IR layers (2),
.IR wm-camera (1)
.SH BUGS
There is no ISP. White balance assumes the picture averages to grey, so
a screenful of one colour comes out washed out, and there is no lens
shading correction, so the corners are dark.
